Accordingly, leave was granted to consider whether the High Court Division has correctly decided the issue as to service of notice of acquisition in the facts of the present case while upholding the decree passed in favour of the plaintiffs.&quot; 

 &quot;The main issue in this suit, therefore, is whether in the absence of a fresh notice under section 5(1a) following the decree in T.S. 36/68, the impugned acquisition could be held to be valid on the basis of notices served in the manner on 23-4-73.&quot; 

 &quot;Having considered the fact of the case carefully we are clearly of the opinion that there was neither any evidence that public notice was not served nor the decree in T.S. 36/68 could be construed to mean as such requiring fresh public notice to be served. Accordingly, the interpretation sought to be given by the High Court Division of the decree in question must be held to be unsustainable. Had the Courts below and the High Court Division considered the matter in their correct perspective they would have found that giving a copy of the old notice under 5(1a) along with fresh notice under 5(3) in 1973 was only in compliance with a misconceived decree but it did not vitiate the fresh notice under 5(3) or the acquisition proceeding in any way&quot; 

 5. Khandker Mahbubuddin Ahmed, learned Advocate for the petitioners, seeking review of the impugned judgment first submits upon referring to section 12C of the Act that this Court has ignored that notice under section 5(1a) contemplates a personal service and the defendants not having proved the same, the acquisition of the petitioners&#39; land cannot be sustained. Secondly, he submits that the judgment and decree in Title Suit No. 36 of 1968 have not been properly construed in that it ought to have been held that it was clearly found in that suit that public notice under section 5(1a) was not served. Thirdly, Mr. Ahmed submits that the observations made as to the &quot;source material&quot; i.e, the order sheet Ext. 1 are not proper and correct. Fourthly, he submits that the impugned judgment has proceeded against one finding of the High Court Division relating to the interpretation given to the decree in Title Suit No. 36 of 1968 with regard to notice under section 5(1a) which may have been rightly held to be unsustainable, but leaving aside that finding, the other part of the judgment of the High Court Division can stand on its own. Lastly, he submits that the question of legal requirement of 15 days&#39; notice under section 5(3) (served in 1973 pursuant to the decree in TS No. 36/68) was not correctly decided.
